    <!-- UNIT 1 LIGHT (Q1-Q4) -->
    <div class="question-card">
        <div class="question-text">1. Which property of water allows it to move upward through vascular plant xylem against gravity?</div>
        <ul class="options-list">
            <li>(A) High specific heat preventing rapid temperature shifts</li>
            <li>(B) Cohesion resulting from hydrogen bonding between adjacent water molecules</li>
            <li>(C) Low density in its solid ice state compared to liquid water</li>
            <li>(D) Covalent bonding between water molecules and cell walls</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="question-card">
        <div class="question-text">2. Which functional group interaction stabilizes the tertiary structure of proteins through covalent bonding?</div>
        <ul class="options-list">
            <li>(A) Hydrogen bonds between carboxyl and amino groups</li>
            <li>(B) Disulfide bridges between cysteine side chains</li>
            <li>(C) Hydrophobic interactions between nonpolar groups</li>
            <li>(D) Ionic bonds between positively and negatively charged R-groups</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="question-card">
        <div class="question-text">3. Which monomeric unit polymerizes to form nucleic acids, and what functional group forms the backbone linkages?</div>
        <ul class="options-list">
            <li>(A) Amino acids linked by peptide bonds</li>
            <li>(B) Monosaccharides linked by glycosidic linkages</li>
            <li>(C) Nucleotides linked by phosphodiester bonds</li>
            <li>(D) Fatty acids linked by ester bonds</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="question-card">
        <div class="question-text">4. What fundamental structural distinction differentiates DNA from RNA?</div>
        <ul class="options-list">
            <li>(A) RNA contains a 2'-hydroxyl group on its ribose sugar; DNA has a 2'-hydrogen on deoxyribose.</li>
            <li>(B) DNA uses uracil instead of thymine.</li>
            <li>(C) RNA is always triple-stranded; DNA is single-stranded.</li>
            <li>(D) DNA lacks a phosphate group in its sugar-phosphate backbone.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- UNIT 2: CELL STRUCTURE & TRANSPORT & CELL CYCLE (Q5-Q16) -->
    <div class="question-card">
        <div class="question-text">5. A cell with a radius of 2 μm is compared to a larger cell with a radius of 6 μm. Calculate the ratio of the surface area-to-volume (SA:V) ratio of the smaller cell to the larger cell.</div>
        <ul class="options-list">
            <li>(A) 1 : 3</li>
            <li>(B) 3 : 1</li>
            <li>(C) 1 : 9</li>
            <li>(D) 9 : 1</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="question-card">
        <div class="question-text">6. Calculate the solute potential (Ψ<sub>s</sub>) of a 0.3 M sucrose solution in an open beaker at 22°C (295 K). (Use R = 0.0831 L bar/mol K, i = 1).</div>
        <ul class="options-list">
            <li>(A) -7.35 bar</li>
            <li>(B) -0.55 bar</li>
            <li>(C) +7.35 bar</li>
            <li>(D) -14.70 bar</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="question-card">
        <div class="question-text">7. A plant cell with a solute potential of -0.8 MPa and pressure potential of 0.2 MPa is placed in an open beaker with a solute potential of -0.3 MPa. What is the net direction of water flow?</div>
        <ul class="options-list">
            <li>(A) Into the cell because Ψ<sub>cell</sub> = -0.6 MPa and Ψ<sub>solution</sub> = -0.3 MPa</li>
            <li>(B) Out of the cell because Ψ<sub>cell</sub> = -1.0 MPa and Ψ<sub>solution</sub> = -0.3 MPa</li>
            <li>(C) No net movement because the system is at equilibrium</li>
            <li>(D) Into the cell because Ψ<sub>cell</sub> = -0.3 MPa and Ψ<sub>solution</sub> = -0.6 MPa</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="question-card">
        <div class="question-text">8. Which cellular organelle contains hydrolytic enzymes responsible for intracellular digestion and recycling damaged organelles?</div>
        <ul class="options-list">
            <li>(A) Peroxisome</li>
            <li>(B) Lysosome</li>
            <li>(C) Golgi apparatus</li>
            <li>(D) Rough Endoplasmic Reticulum</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="question-card">
        <div class="question-text">9. Which organelle is primarily responsible for lipid synthesis, carbohydrate metabolism, and drug detoxification in liver cells?</div>
        <ul class="options-list">
            <li>(A) Rough Endoplasmic Reticulum</li>
            <li>(B) Smooth Endoplasmic Reticulum</li>
            <li>(C) Nucleolus</li>
            <li>(D) Ribosome</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="question-card">
        <div class="question-text">10. How does cholesterol maintain plasma membrane fluidity at varying temperatures in animal cells?</div>
        <ul class="options-list">
            <li>(A) It permanently solidifies the lipid bilayer at high temperatures.</li>
            <li>(B) It hinders phospholipid packing at low temperatures and restricts movement at high temperatures.</li>
            <li>(C) It increases membrane permeability to polar molecules at low temperatures.</li>
            <li>(D) It actively pumps ions across the lipid bilayer.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="question-card">
        <div class="question-text">11. In an experiment, 100 cells in a culture are examined during interphase and mitosis. If 80 cells are in interphase, 10 in prophase, 4 in metaphase, 4 in anaphase, and 2 in telophase, and the total cell cycle length is 20 hours, estimate the time spent in metaphase.</div>
        <ul class="options-list">
            <li>(A) 0.8 hours (48 minutes)</li>
            <li>(B) 1.6 hours (96 minutes)</li>
            <li>(C) 2.0 hours (120 minutes)</li>
            <li>(D) 4.0 hours (240 minutes)</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="question-card">
        <div class="question-text">12. Which event occurs during the S phase of the eukaryotic cell cycle?</div>
        <ul class="options-list">
            <li>(A) Separation of sister chromatids</li>
            <li>(B) Replication of nuclear DNA</li>
            <li>(C) Breakdown of the nuclear envelope</li>
            <li>(D) Cytokinesis via cleavage furrow formation</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="question-card">
        <div class="question-text">13. What directly causes the activation of Cyclin-Dependent Kinases (CDKs) to propel the cell past the G2/M checkpoint?</div>
        <ul class="options-list">
            <li>(A) Degradation of ATP</li>
            <li>(B) Binding of cyclin proteins whose concentration accumulates during G2</li>
            <li>(C) Phosphorylation of DNA polymerase</li>
            <li>(D) Dephosphorylation of histone proteins</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="question-card">
        <div class="question-text">14. What occurs during Anaphase of mitosis?</div>
        <ul class="options-list">
            <li>(A) Chromosomes align at the metaphase plate.</li>
            <li>(B) Sister chromatids separate and move toward opposite poles.</li>
            <li>(C) Nuclear membrane reforms around chromatin.</li>
            <li>(D) Chromatin condenses into visible chromosomes.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="question-card">
        <div class="question-text">15. Cancer cells often bypass the G1 checkpoint (restriction point) due to which of the following molecular alterations?</div>
        <ul class="options-list">
            <li>(A) Overexpression of tumor suppressor gene p53</li>
            <li>(B) Loss-of-function mutation in proteins that inhibit CDK-cyclin complexes</li>
            <li>(C) Inability to synthesize ATP</li>
            <li>(D) Continuous activation of apoptosis pathways</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="question-card">
        <div class="question-text">16. Which transport mechanism moves sodium ions (Na<sup>+</sup>) out of the cell and potassium ions (K<sup>+</sup>) into the cell against their electrochemical gradients?</div>
        <ul class="options-list">
            <li>(A) Simple diffusion</li>
            <li>(B) Facilitated diffusion through ion channels</li>
            <li>(C) Primary active transport via the Na<sup>+</sup>/K<sup>+</sup> ATPase pump</li>
            <li>(D) Osmosis</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- UNIT 3: CELLULAR ENERGETICS (Q17-Q30) -->
    <div class="question-card">
        <div class="question-text">17. An enzyme catalyzes a reaction converting 15 mg of substrate into product in 5 minutes. Calculate the rate of reaction in μg/sec.</div>
        <ul class="options-list">
            <li>(A) 3.0 μg/sec</li>
            <li>(B) 50.0 μg/sec</li>
            <li>(C) 300.0 μg/sec</li>
            <li>(D) 3000.0 μg/sec</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="question-card">
        <div class="question-text">18. An enzyme's activity was measured at 25°C (1.2 mmol/min) and 35°C (2.4 mmol/min). Calculate the Q<sub>10</sub> temperature coefficient for this enzyme. (Q<sub>10</sub> = (R<sub>2</sub>/R<sub>1</sub>)<sup>10/(T<sub>2</sub>-T<sub>1</sub>)</sup>).</div>
        <ul class="options-list">
            <li>(A) 1.5</li>
            <li>(B) 2.0</li>
            <li>(C) 2.5</li>
            <li>(D) 3.0</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="question-card">
        <div class="question-text">19. How does a competitive inhibitor alter enzyme kinetics?</div>
        <ul class="options-list">
            <li>(A) Decreases V<sub>max</sub> without changing K<sub>m</sub></li>
            <li>(B) Binds allosterically, preventing substrate binding permanently</li>
            <li>(C) Binds to the active site, increasing apparent K<sub>m</sub> while V<sub>max</sub> can still be reached at high substrate concentration</li>
            <li>(D) Irreversibly denatures the tertiary structure of the enzyme</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="question-card">
        <div class="question-text">20. Which statement accurately describes the second law of thermodynamics in biological systems?</div>
        <ul class="options-list">
            <li>(A) Energy cannot be created or destroyed.</li>
            <li>(B) Every energy transfer increases the total entropy (disorder) of the universe.</li>
            <li>(C) Organisms are closed systems that do not exchange energy with their environment.</li>
            <li>(D) Anabolic reactions decrease overall universal entropy without heat production.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="question-card">
        <div class="question-text">21. What is the ultimate source of electrons that replaces those excited and lost from Photosystem II (P680) during light-dependent reactions?</div>
        <ul class="options-list">
            <li>(A) Carbon dioxide (CO<sub>2</sub>)</li>
            <li>(B) Water (H<sub>2</sub>O)</li>
            <li>(C) NADPH</li>
            <li>(D) Glucose</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="question-card">
        <div class="question-text">22. Where do the light-independent reactions (Calvin Cycle) of photosynthesis occur in eukaryotic autotrophs?</div>
        <ul class="options-list">
            <li>(A) Thylakoid lumen</li>
            <li>(B) Thylakoid membrane</li>
            <li>(C) Stroma of the chloroplast</li>
            <li>(D) Outer chloroplast membrane</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="question-card">
        <div class="question-text">23. What primary products of the light-dependent reactions are consumed by the Calvin Cycle to reduce 3-phosphoglycerate into G3P?</div>
        <ul class="options-list">
            <li>(A) O<sub>2</sub> and ATP</li>
            <li>(B) ATP and NADPH</li>
            <li>(C) CO<sub>2</sub> and H<sub>2</sub>O</li>
            <li>(D) NADP<sup>+</sup> and ADP</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="question-card">
        <div class="question-text">24. What directly drives ATP synthesis via ATP synthase during oxidative phosphorylation in mitochondria?</div>
        <ul class="options-list">
            <li>(A) Direct transfer of phosphate from pyruvate to ADP</li>
            <li>(B) Proton flow down its electrochemical gradient from intermembrane space to matrix</li>
            <li>(C) Oxidation of glucose directly in the mitochondrial matrix</li>
            <li>(D) Absorption of light photons by cytochrome complexes</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="question-card">
        <div class="question-text">25. In aerobic cellular respiration, which process yields the majority of ATP per molecule of oxidized glucose?</div>
        <ul class="options-list">
            <li>(A) Glycolysis</li>
            <li>(B) Pyruvate oxidation</li>
            <li>(C) Citric Acid (Krebs) Cycle</li>
            <li>(D) Oxidative Phosphorylation (ETC + Chemiosmosis)</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="question-card">
        <div class="question-text">26. What is the net metabolic yield of glycolysis per molecule of glucose?</div>
        <ul class="options-list">
            <li>(A) 2 Pyruvate, 2 ATP (net), 2 NADH</li>
            <li>(B) 2 Acetyl-CoA, 4 ATP, 2 FADH<sub>2</sub></li>
            <li>(C) 1 Pyruvate, 32 ATP, 6 NADH</li>
            <li>(D) 2 Lactate, 36 ATP, 0 NADH</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="question-card">
        <div class="question-text">27. What function does fermentation serve in animal muscle cells under anaerobic conditions?</div>
        <ul class="options-list">
            <li>(A) Generates large amounts of additional ATP via substrate-level phosphorylation</li>
            <li>(B) Regenerates NAD<sup>+</sup> from NADH to allow glycolysis to continue</li>
            <li>(C) Converts lactate back into oxygen for mitochondrial respiration</li>
            <li>(D) Produces ethanol and CO<sub>2</sub> to maintain cytosolic pH</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="question-card">
        <div class="question-text">28. Which mitochondrial location accumulates protons (H<sup>+</sup>) to form the high electrochemical gradient during electron transport chain operation?</div>
        <ul class="options-list">
            <li>(A) Mitochondrial matrix</li>
            <li>(B) Intermembrane space</li>
            <li>(C) Outer mitochondrial membrane</li>
            <li>(D) Cytosol</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="question-card">
        <div class="question-text">29. An chemical compound acts as an uncoupler of oxidative phosphorylation by carrying protons across the inner mitochondrial membrane. What effect will this have on ATP synthesis and oxygen consumption?</div>
        <ul class="options-list">
            <li>(A) Both ATP synthesis and oxygen consumption will increase.</li>
            <li>(B) ATP synthesis decreases while oxygen consumption continues/increases, producing heat.</li>
            <li>(C) ATP synthesis increases while oxygen consumption stops.</li>
            <li>(D) Both ATP synthesis and oxygen consumption completely stop immediately.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="question-card">
        <div class="question-text">30. Rubisco (Ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate carboxylase-oxygenase) can catalyze a wasteful side reaction with oxygen (O<sub>2</sub>) instead of CO<sub>2</sub>. What is this process called?</div>
        <ul class="options-list">
            <li>(A) Cyclic photophosphorylation</li>
            <li>(B) Photorespiration</li>
            <li>(C) Lactic acid fermentation</li>
            <li>(D) Oxidative decarboxylation</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

